  IME reaching zero, and lower, AC as soon as possible is the far and
away the best method for starting spellcasters to survive in combat.

  Most spells aren't used directly in combat anyway.  Off-line casting
is easy and fairly safe if you get in a closet, close the door, and do
some Elbereths.

  When my starting wizards find non-cursed mithril, they put it on.
When they find a non-cursed shield, they put it on.  Armor can be
removed much more easily than hitpoints can be regained.

  Few early characters can survive strictly by spellcasting.  It seems
like a fairly advanced techique even then.  Better armor will help
almost any early character survive.  

  Plus, get enough enchanted accessory armor: boots, shield, helm,
gloves and you can wear (studded) leather body armor.  Studded leather
is only two points less protection than elven mithril, and only 50
heavier.  

  You can then just take one turn to remove the shield, and another turn
to put it back on.  If that's not enough for low failure rates, remove
the helm in one turn.   

  And remember, I'm not just talking about in combat here.  

  - Spell of haste self cast off-line lasts and can be pumped up.  
  - Ditto invisibility.  
  - Jumping can get you away from a potentially bad situation in a 
    hurry.  (There's a reason they're called escape spells.)
  - Light let's you see monsters coming.  
  - Detect Monsters shows you all monsters on the level.  
  - Charm Monster turns bloodthirsty, slavering monsters into 
    bloodthirsty, slavering monsters that are on your side.
  - ad nauseum.



  Spell-vicious armor does not have to stop spell-casting completely.
Not that I think foregoing spellcasting completely early on is a bad
idea.  

  Early spellcasters tend to be oysters that should be looking for a
nice strong shell.

